What is Deployment?
Simple Definition

Deployment is a Kubernetes object used to manage Pods. 
It provides self-healing, scaling, rolling updates, and rollback functionality by maintaining the desired number of Pod replicas through a ReplicaSet.

What is Self-Healing?

Self-healing means:

If a Pod crashes, gets deleted, or becomes unhealthy, Kubernetes automatically creates a new Pod to replace it.

$kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=3
$kubectl get deploy
$kubectl get rs
$kubectl get pods
$kubectl get pods -w
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rolling Update

Rolling Update is a Kubernetes deployment strategy used to update an application without downtime. When a new version is deployed, Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually launches new Pods while terminating old Pods one by one. This ensures the application remains available throughout the update process. If the new version has issues, Kubernetes can easily roll back to the previous version. 
Rolling updates are managed by Deployments and provide zero or minimal downtime during application upgrades.
$kubectl set image deployment/nginx-demo nginx=nginx:1.25

$kubectl set image deployment/nginx-demo nginx=nginx:1.25

$kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-demo

$kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-demo

$kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-demo

Step 1
Create Deployment

Step 2:
Deployment creates ReplicaSet

Step 3:
ReplicaSet creates Pods

Step 4:
Pods start Containers



